{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And he sent his brothers and they went.</b> All his brothers — Reuven and Shimon and all the rest, with Binyamin included among them. Now Yehudah fulfilled his condition in what he had vowed and banned himself: 'if I do not bring him to you, then I have sinned to you.' And he was punished for this, as the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said: all those forty years that Israel stood in the wilderness, the bones of Yehudah were rolling in the ark, because that vow was dependent on the hands of others and the matter was in doubt for him whether it would be fulfilled or not, and therefore he was punished even though he returned him to his father. From here the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, learned: a ban imposed on condition requires release (hatarah) even if the condition is fulfilled. And so they expounded in tractate Makkot, chapter 'And these are the go'el' (11b): Rav Yehudah said in the name of Rav: a ban on condition requires release — from where do we know it? From Yehudah, as it is said: 'if I do not bring him to you and set him before you,' etc." "<b>Do not be agitated on the way.</b> Do not be afraid — from the language of (Deuteronomy 28) 'ולב רגז'; (Deuteronomy 2) 'ורגזו וחלו מפניך.' And since it is the way of the world that carriers of grain and bread and food in days of famine are fearful — how much more so when they return with all their property and would not hurry along — therefore he told them to go speedily and hurry to come, as he said: 'make haste and go up to my father,' and not to be afraid on the way at all, for his name was upon them — he who rules over all the land of Egypt, and the life of all those lands is in his hand, and from his terror all will fear, and they would go and come in peace. This is the language of the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded</b> 'do not be agitated on the way' — do not engage in a matter of halakhah because of the exertion of the road. And he did not prevent them from anything except deep investigation, because of the exertion, but he did not prevent them from studying at all, heaven forbid, for the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said: two scholars walking on the road who have no words of Torah between them are worthy of being burned, as it is said (II Kings 2): 'ויהי המה הולכים הלוך ודבר והנה רכב אש וסוסי אש ויפרידו בין שניהם' — because they were speaking words of Torah, 'and they separated them'; had they not been speaking, they would have been burned."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:37.538740098Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}